solution:
**Given:**
N = 125; plan n = 20, Ac = 2 (code F, AQL 4%); rectifying inspection; process quality currently p1 = 5.8%, deteriorated case p2 = 7.4%.

**Step 1:** The quoted plan: code F, n = 20, Ac = 2, Re = 3 — inspect 20 units per lot, accept if at most 2 nonconforming items are found; rejected lots are fully screened and made conforming (rectification).

**Step 2:** Acceptance probability at the current process quality p1 = 0.058 (X1 ~ Binomial(20, 0.058)).
P(X = 0) = C(20, 0) * 0.058^0 * 0.942^20 = 0.3027;
P(X = 1) = C(20, 1) * 0.058^1 * 0.942^19 = 0.3728;
P(X = 2) = C(20, 2) * 0.058^2 * 0.942^18 = 0.2180;
Pa(p1) = 0.3027 + 0.3728 + 0.2180 = 0.8935

**Step 3:** Acceptance probability at the deteriorated process quality p2 = 0.074 (X2 ~ Binomial(20, 0.074)).
P(X = 0) = C(20, 0) * 0.074^0 * 0.926^20 = 0.2149;
P(X = 1) = C(20, 1) * 0.074^1 * 0.926^19 = 0.3435;
P(X = 2) = C(20, 2) * 0.074^2 * 0.926^18 = 0.2607;
Pa(p2) = 0.2149 + 0.3435 + 0.2607 = 0.8191

**Step 4:** Average outgoing quality. Under the stated policy a rejected lot is screened 100% and leaves with no nonconforming units at all, while an accepted lot leaves with them only among the 125 - 20 = 105 units never inspected (those found in the sample were replaced). A fraction Pa of lots therefore carries about p*(125 - 20) nonconforming units in 125, giving AOQ = Pa*p*(N - n)/N.
AOQ(p1) = 0.8935 * 0.058 * (125 - 20)/125 * 10^6 = 43531 ppm;  AOQ(p2) = 0.8191 * 0.074 * (125 - 20)/125 * 10^6 = 50915 ppm (each to the nearest whole ppm).
AOQ(p2) is the larger: although fewer lots pass at p2 (Pa falls from 0.8935 to 0.8191), the lots that do pass carry a higher fraction nonconforming, and that effect outweighs the extra screening of rejected lots — the deteriorated process leaves the worse outgoing quality.

**Step 5:** Average total inspection at the current quality. Every submitted lot costs the 20 sampled units; the fraction 1 - Pa that is rejected costs the other 105 as well, so ATI = n + (1 - Pa)*(N - n).
ATI = n + (1 - Pa(p1))*(N - n) = 20 + (1 - 0.8935) * (125 - 20) = 31.2 units per lot

**Answer:** The average total inspection per lot at the current quality is 31.2 units
